WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:08.280
- Hi everyone. I am Maurice Samuels,

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.200
the Director of the Yale program

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.090
for the study of Anti-Semitism,

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:14.340
and it's my great pleasure to welcome you

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:19.290
to the first event of the
2023-24 academic year.

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:21.540
I was on leave last year, which was great,

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:26.220
but I'm also very happy to
be back hosting these events.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.290
This year, some events
are gonna be on Zoom

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:30.810
and some are gonna be in person,

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:33.810
but we're going to be
broadcasting the live events

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:35.010
on Zoom as well.

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:40.010
So I urge everyone to check
out the YPSA calendar of events

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:44.040
on our website and get
on the mailing list.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:47.610
I'm especially excited to
introduce today's speaker,

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:51.060
Martha Hodes since she
was the Interim Director

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:53.280
of the Doris and Lewis B. Cullman Center

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.160
for Scholars and Writers at
the New York Public Library

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.920
where I had my fellowship last year.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.890
So I had the chance to
have many conversations

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:04.863
with Martha as she finished this book.

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:08.250
She's now back to her regular day job

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:11.160
as Professor of History at NYU

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:15.570
where she specializes in American
history of the Civil War.

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:17.220
She's the author of many books

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:19.747
including "White Women, Black Men:

00:01:19.747 --> 00:01:23.077
"Illicit Sex in the
Nineteenth-Century South,"

00:01:23.077 --> 00:01:26.520
"The Sea Captain's Wife: A
True Story of Love, Race,

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.120
and War in the Nineteenth
Century," and "Mourning Lincoln,"

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.630
which won the Lincoln Prize in 2016.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:37.050
Actually, all of her books have won awards

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:39.300
and made many Best Book of the Year lists

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:40.830
when they were published.

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:44.070
Martha is a past recipient
of the Guggenheim Fellowship

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:47.430
and has many other distinctions
that I won't attempt to list

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:49.740
because I'm so eager
to hear her talk about

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:53.340
her fascinating new book, "My Hijacking:

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.700
A Personal History of
Forgetting and Remembering"

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:00.180
published by Harper
Collins this past spring.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:01.830
Before I hand it over to Martha,

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:05.640
let me just explain how
things will work today.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:10.350
Martha is gonna speak for about
35 or so minutes, I think.

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:13.590
Then we'll open it up to
questions from the audience.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:16.830
Please submit your questions to me using

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:20.220
the Q&amp;A function at the
bottom of your screen

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:24.180
and I'll do my best to ask
as many of them as I can.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:26.550
We can't see or hear you however,

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:28.950
and the chat function is disabled.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:32.100
So please only use the Q&amp;A function

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.340
and you can send questions
as soon as you think of them.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:37.470
You don't have to wait
for the talk to be over,

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:40.170
you can just start sending them to me.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.150
Okay, so that's it for the housekeeping

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:45.300
and I will now hand it over to Martha.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:46.980
- Thank you, thank you so much Maury

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.590
and thank you for this opportunity
to present to this group,

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:51.390
and I thank everybody for tuning in

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:53.460
everyone who's with us today.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:56.520
Well, as maybe was clear
from Maury's introduction,

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:59.160
I've never written a book
like this one, "My Hijacking:

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:01.800
A Personal History of
Forgetting and Remembering."

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:05.040
I'm a historian of the
19th century United States

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:09.060
and all my other books focus
on Race and the Civil War era,

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.700
the most recent, a book
about personal responses

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:13.233
to Lincoln's assassination.

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:17.160
My Hijacking like my other books,

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:19.830
is based on deep archival research,

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:23.820
but here, I also
intertwine my own memories

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:26.433
and my own research into family history.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.760
This book, for those of
you who haven't read it,

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:34.230
is about the hijacking of
three planes in September 1970,

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:35.760
the hijackers were members

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:38.640
of the Popular Front for
the Liberation of Palestine,

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:42.150
a radical Marxist-Leninist
faction, too radical

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:46.080
even for the Palestine
Liberation Organization, the PLO.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:48.270
I was 12 years old at the time,

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:50.670
a passenger on one of the planes.

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:53.940
I was traveling with my
13-year-old sister Catherine,

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:56.880
and we were unaccompanied by adults.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:00.810
We were returning to
New York from Tel Aviv.

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:03.510
My mother had moved to
Israel to help start

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:05.790
the Batsheva Dance Company
under the direction

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:06.623
of Martha Graham.

00:04:06.623 --> 00:04:10.200
My parents were both former
principal Graham dancers

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.680
and my sister and I spent our
childhood summers in Israel

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:16.953
and I'm just going to share
a couple of images with you.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.700
That's me on the left
in the summer of 1970

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:26.520
at my mother's Tel Aviv
apartment building,

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:29.490
and then my sister
Catherine and me practicing

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:31.110
our yoga poses on the beach.

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:34.350
And that photo on the right
was taken the day before

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:36.213
we boarded the plane home.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.820
Catherine and I were held
hostage in the Jordan Desert

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:47.400
inside the airplane for a week.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:50.820
Our captors hope to exchange
the passengers and crew

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:52.503
for Palestinian prisoners.

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:55.830
The book then is something of a hybrid

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:58.590
about my own participation if unwitting

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:01.350
in a world historical event.

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:03.180
What I'd like to do this afternoon is,

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:06.360
is offer to you various
parts of the manuscript

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:10.770
with interjections for
my listeners here today.

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:14.163
So to begin, I will set
the scene at the outset.

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:17.100
Sunday before sunrise,

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:20.403
everything is dark and quiet
in our Tel Aviv neighborhood.

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:24.480
We packed the night before and
now my mother helps us close

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:27.363
the tops of our identical gray suitcases.

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:31.860
At LAD airport, not much
more than a single building,

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:33.570
we come to an ascending stairway

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:37.230
with a big BON VOYAGE sign hanging above.

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:39.990
From there, travelers must proceed alone,

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:41.790
and when we arrive at that stairway,

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:44.310
my mother puts on her sunglasses.

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:46.890
She's crying because she
has to say goodbye to us

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:47.943
for a long time.

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:50.940
I like that memory.

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:53.580
Something about it
parallels my favorite book

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:55.353
in the summer of 1970.

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:59.700
My Israeli stepfather had
given us "The Little Prince"

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:01.830
with an intriguing cover illustration

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:04.320
of a young boy standing on a barren planet

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:06.660
with a miniature volcano.

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:08.880
First my stepfather read the story to us,

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:10.590
then I read it to myself,

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:13.380
enchanted by the lonely aviator narrator

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:15.630
whose plane crashed in the desert

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:19.050
and the downcast boy
wanderer he met there.

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:21.600
That boy, the little prince fits farewell

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:26.017
to a temperamental flower,
a rose he loves very much.

00:06:26.017 --> 00:06:27.060
"Of course, I love you."

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:28.687
The rose tells the little prince.

00:06:28.687 --> 00:06:31.707
"It is my fault that you have
not known it all the while."

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:34.530
She implores the boy to be happy

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:36.630
and asks him not to linger

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:38.913
for she did not want
him to see her crying.

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:43.230
I read the book twice more
until I had it nearly memorized.

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:45.933
In my diary I wrote, it is fantastic!

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:48.870
In various parts of the book then,

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:51.660
I interweave quotations
from the Little Prince,

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:54.000
which appear in the text in parentheses,

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:56.880
and you'll hear some of
these in the next while

00:06:56.880 --> 00:06:58.143
as I'm speaking with you.

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:02.013
To continue from a bit farther on,

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:07.800
takeoff from Frankfurt is
right on schedule at 11:02 AM,

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:10.920
less than an hour later
as we soar over Belgium,

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:13.800
the captain announces an
altitude of 28,000 feet

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:16.680
with Brussels visible on the left.

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:19.830
I hear a commotion
shouting that sounds angry,

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:21.870
words I don't understand.

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:24.090
Now a woman is running up the aisle,

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:26.073
a man follows both of them shouting.

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:29.130
Some passengers think a husband and wife

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:30.840
are having a violent argument,

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:33.480
others that the woman is
airsick running to the bathroom

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:34.313
to vomit.

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:39.540
I see only a blur, but
Catherine sees the man's gun.

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:41.910
Others see the nickel plated revolver too

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:44.550
along with the woman's finger
inserted through the ring

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:46.207
of a hand grenade.

00:07:46.207 --> 00:07:47.670
"I hope it's not the Arabs"

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:50.400
gasps the old lady sitting in our row.

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:53.790
Clutching her heart, she
moans, "My pills! My pills!"

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:56.850
prompting Catherine to
rummage through her handbag.

00:07:56.850 --> 00:07:59.250
The old lady warns my sister who's crying

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:02.640
as she searches for the medicine
to take off the Jewish star

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:05.163
hanging on a slim gold
chain around her neck.

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:08.190
The purser who still thinks
a man and woman are fighting

00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:10.680
with each other and he runs
after them clapping a hand

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:13.860
on the man's shoulder, the
man turns, points his gun

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:16.237
and shouts, "Get back! Get back!"

00:08:16.237 --> 00:08:17.917
"Oh my God," the purser cries out.

00:08:17.917 --> 00:08:19.500
"I don't believe it."

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:21.300
Just in front of the curtain that leads

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:22.890
to the first class cabin,

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:25.477
the man and woman turn to
face the passengers shouting,

00:08:25.477 --> 00:08:27.207
"Hijack! Hijack!"

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:31.170
People speak urgently if quietly,

00:08:31.170 --> 00:08:33.450
some exchanging phone numbers and pledging

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:36.873
to notify family members
should only some of us survive.

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:40.560
Soon a woman's voice comes
over the loud speaker,

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:42.843
which I record this way in my diary.

00:08:43.717 --> 00:08:45.750
"Good afternoon, ladies and gentlemen.

00:08:45.750 --> 00:08:49.500
I'm the new pilot who has taken
command of your TWA flight.

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:50.670
Keep calm.

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:53.577
Please cooperate and put
your hands behind your head."

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:57.690
Catherine and I copy the
other passengers raising arms

00:08:57.690 --> 00:08:59.220
and lacing fingers.

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:01.770
I wonder why the woman doesn't
tell us to put our hands

00:09:01.770 --> 00:09:04.530
over our heads the way robbers do,

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:06.360
but she isn't trying to
prevent the passengers

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:07.770
from drawing weapons.

00:09:07.770 --> 00:09:10.500
Instead, she needs all
of us to brace for impact

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:12.723
in case something goes
wrong in the cockpit.

00:09:14.370 --> 00:09:17.160
The landing is a greaser,
very smooth with no damage

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:18.660
to the aircraft.

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:22.533
Local time is 6:40 PM about
a quarter hour before sunset,

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:26.340
I let Catherine appraise the situation.

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:30.090
Leaning past me, she peers out
the window at billows of dust

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:31.833
and sand, which look like smoke.

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:36.060
Commandos on the ground have
fashioned a makeshift runway

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:40.080
by lining up barrels filled
with sand and diesel-soaked rags

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:43.560
and the blazing torches light
up people in military uniforms

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:45.063
which make it look like a war.

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:48.990
Someone props a ladder
from the back of a truck

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:51.300
up to the planes front exit.

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:53.850
People dressed in fatigues,
ascend the ladder,

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:55.830
armed with flashlights and rifles,

00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:58.020
lanterns and machine guns.

00:09:58.020 --> 00:10:01.020
Some are boys no more
than 14 or 15 years old

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:03.213
and some are young women with short hair.

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:07.170
The men and women who took
over our flight climbed down,

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:09.930
triumphant heroes welcomed
by their comrades,

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:13.503
melting into the outdoor
throng never to be identified.

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:16.470
Listening to my captors,

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:20.100
I absorb both apology and explanation.

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:22.020
In one imprinted memory,

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:25.140
I watch commandos carry
thick yellow cables.

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:27.300
The faces of the men expressionless

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:29.730
as they go about their task.

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:33.150
Although I determine somehow
that this is dynamite,

00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:36.030
there's no memory of accompanying emotion,

00:10:36.030 --> 00:10:39.900
only the sense of a child
intently watching every move.

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:42.600
Again, I'm going to share a
couple of images with you.

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:49.170
You can see here two of
the three hijacked planes

00:10:49.170 --> 00:10:52.680
in the Jordan Desert, our
TWA airliner on the left

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:55.380
and the Swiss airplane on the right

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:58.470
in between flies the Palestinian flag.

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:03.150
A few days later, a British
plane, BOAC would join us.

00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:05.733
Here you can see the three
planes in the desert.

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:10.830
When I began writing this book,

00:11:10.830 --> 00:11:13.630
many of my memories of the
hijacking were pleasant ones.

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:16.770
One day when Catherine's diary was resting

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:19.770
on her tray table, one of
the commandos spied the heart

00:11:19.770 --> 00:11:21.780
she'd drawn on the back cover

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:24.780
with the name of the Israeli
boy she liked that summer,

00:11:24.780 --> 00:11:27.987
eyes twinkling, the man
exclaimed, "Romantic."

00:11:29.010 --> 00:11:31.710
Once when our captors let us outside,

00:11:31.710 --> 00:11:33.390
one of the commandos joined the children

00:11:33.390 --> 00:11:35.400
in a game of jump rope, belt clanging

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:36.873
and military boots thumping.

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:40.860
Another day sitting in the
shade under an airplane wing,

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:42.907
someone started a round of John Denver's,

00:11:42.907 --> 00:11:44.520
"Leaving on a Jet Plane,"

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:47.130
which the folk trio, Peter,
Paul, and Mary had made into

00:11:47.130 --> 00:11:49.050
a hit single that year.

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:52.080
Loud voices among the singing
hostages changed the words

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:54.000
to living on a jet plane,

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:56.160
which Catherine and I
thought was hilarious

00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:58.687
and everyone was laughing
as we sang the line,

00:11:58.687 --> 00:12:00.777
"Don't know when I'll be back again."

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:07.140
In the book I write about
the time in my adult life,

00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:09.843
when I began to think
about the hijacking again.

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:14.130
It began right after 9/11
and here I hasten to add

00:12:14.130 --> 00:12:15.450
that there's no straight line

00:12:15.450 --> 00:12:18.600
from the 1970 hijackings to 9/11.

00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:19.920
After all, the Popular Front

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:22.260
was a Marxist-Leninist organization,

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:24.063
they were not Muslim Jihadists.

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:27.150
Nonetheless, a day of multiple hijackings,

00:12:27.150 --> 00:12:31.140
understandably unburied
memories and feelings.

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:33.540
Soon after 9/11, I was on
my way to the University

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:35.040
of Michigan to give a lecture.

00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:39.690
I alerted my host that
I reserved the right

00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:42.300
to turn around on the jet way
if I couldn't bring myself

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:43.860
to board the plane.

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:45.860
A warning she accepted without question.

00:12:47.340 --> 00:12:49.470
There was no need to reveal anything more

00:12:49.470 --> 00:12:52.620
since in October 2001,
people all over the world

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:53.763
were afraid to fly.

00:12:54.750 --> 00:12:59.250
Some on the flight cried
quietly, others prayed visibly.

00:12:59.250 --> 00:13:00.900
For the first time in 30 years,

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:05.340
other people were acting the
way I always felt on airplanes,

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:09.090
and that's when memories of
the hijacking began to intrude.

00:13:09.090 --> 00:13:12.480
Right away, I wrote down
everything I could conjure.

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:15.150
It was all murky and there wasn't much.

00:13:15.150 --> 00:13:17.823
And for the first time
ever, I wanted to know more.

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:21.450
Telling the stories of our own lives.

00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:24.810
How can we answer the questions
we think to ask later on

00:13:24.810 --> 00:13:26.883
after the passage of so much time?

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:31.890
Historian by trade, I wanted
to do more than excavate

00:13:31.890 --> 00:13:33.363
just my own memories.

00:13:34.410 --> 00:13:37.410
With a scholar's passion
for evidence and accuracy,

00:13:37.410 --> 00:13:41.310
I wanted to compare
recollections, compare documents,

00:13:41.310 --> 00:13:44.193
and compare recollections and
documents with each other.

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:49.440
As a hostage, I had quelled
memories and emotions.

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:53.580
As a historian, I wanted to
search for facts and feelings

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:56.430
to provide meaning for everything
that had happened to me

00:13:56.430 --> 00:13:57.423
and my family.

00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:00.870
I dug farther into my own memories

00:14:00.870 --> 00:14:03.270
and I put my memories
together with Catherine's.

00:14:04.410 --> 00:14:06.750
I began to talk with my elderly parents,

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:10.470
taking note of my father's
carefully scripted stories,

00:14:10.470 --> 00:14:13.380
my stepmother's more nuanced versions,

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:15.333
and my mother's misty memories.

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:20.040
I found mementos my father had
saved, some in my old room,

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:22.923
some in his self-storage
unit over on 11th Avenue.

00:14:24.210 --> 00:14:25.980
I talked with childhood friends,

00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:27.990
rekindling snuffed out conversations

00:14:27.990 --> 00:14:30.423
about a topic I hadn't
mentioned in decades.

00:14:31.740 --> 00:14:33.060
I asked friends from high school,

00:14:33.060 --> 00:14:35.850
college and beyond if
I'd ever even told them

00:14:35.850 --> 00:14:36.963
I'd been hijacked.

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:40.440
I visited archives, read news coverage,

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:42.810
and watched television broadcasts.

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:45.000
I read the manifestos of my captors

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:48.300
and listened to their
narratives, past and present.

00:14:48.300 --> 00:14:51.240
I met and conversed with fellow hostages.

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:53.840
I went back to the places
where everything happened.

00:14:54.750 --> 00:14:59.100
I wondered, if I researched
the hijacking as a historian,

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:02.100
could that stamp out the absurd sensation

00:15:02.100 --> 00:15:05.460
that none of it had ever
happened to me at all?

00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:09.000
Could recovering the feelings
that accompanied the hijacking

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:11.733
make real something that felt so unreal.

00:15:13.710 --> 00:15:15.780
Inside the plane, I had my diary with me

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:17.250
and I wrote every day,

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:19.320
setting down a little
over a thousand words

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:20.703
during my desert sojourn.

00:15:21.570 --> 00:15:23.400
Historians prize personal writings

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:26.880
that date from the time and
place under investigation.

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:27.990
And when I started out,

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:29.760
I was confident that my diary

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:31.860
would be my trusted scaffolding

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:35.460
and in the moment account of
everything that came to pass

00:15:35.460 --> 00:15:38.400
along with my thoughts and
feelings up in the air,

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:41.553
in the desert at release and upon return.

00:15:43.080 --> 00:15:46.230
In 1970, I named my journal Claire,

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:48.840
writing my entries in the form of letters.

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:51.120
In that technique, I copied Anne Frank,

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:54.750
who began her diary
entries with "Dear Kitty."

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:57.030
Anne Frank and I both wanted to be writers

00:15:57.030 --> 00:15:59.043
and her birthday was the same as mine.

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:04.350
With a combination of ingenuousness
and self-consciousness,

00:16:04.350 --> 00:16:06.840
I could be an observant 12-year-old,

00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:09.510
but rereading my diary
so many years later,

00:16:09.510 --> 00:16:12.240
it became clear that during
that week in September,

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:15.720
I had constrained my
powers of observation.

00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:18.420
So much happened that I never recorded.

00:16:18.420 --> 00:16:22.230
It was as if I'd named my
diary Claire, meaning clear,

00:16:22.230 --> 00:16:24.570
as a way to fool myself
that I wrote my entries

00:16:24.570 --> 00:16:25.953
with any transparency.

00:16:27.510 --> 00:16:28.830
I never wrote about the gun I saw

00:16:28.830 --> 00:16:31.320
in the co-pilot's neck up in the air.

00:16:31.320 --> 00:16:33.480
I never wrote about the
commandos I watched wiring

00:16:33.480 --> 00:16:36.510
or are playing with dynamite
the night we landed.

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:37.620
I never wrote about the time

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:40.520
one of the women commandos
pointed her gun straight at me.

00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:43.140
When my history students work

00:16:43.140 --> 00:16:45.930
with first-person historical
documents, whether diaries,

00:16:45.930 --> 00:16:48.030
letters or courtroom testimony,

00:16:48.030 --> 00:16:50.850
I prompt them to ask, "Why did this person

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:52.887
tell this story this way?"

00:16:54.030 --> 00:16:56.430
Rereading my diary while writing the book,

00:16:56.430 --> 00:17:00.090
I saw that the aspiring
writer in me had constructed

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:04.380
not a full record, but
instead a tolerable story.

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:07.530
Not an honest story, but instead a story

00:17:07.530 --> 00:17:09.453
I could tell when I got home.

00:17:10.320 --> 00:17:13.710
That narrative of omissions
would comprise my version

00:17:13.710 --> 00:17:16.620
of the hijacking and I would
carry it with me for years

00:17:16.620 --> 00:17:17.823
and years afterward.

00:17:19.740 --> 00:17:22.470
Unsurprisingly then in
the course of my research,

00:17:22.470 --> 00:17:24.810
I became aware of
aspects of the hijacking,

00:17:24.810 --> 00:17:28.110
far less pleasant than
much of what I remembered.

00:17:28.110 --> 00:17:31.170
For one thing, there was a
stream of weapon-laden visitors

00:17:31.170 --> 00:17:34.410
who filed up and down the
narrow aircraft aisle.

00:17:34.410 --> 00:17:36.720
Some pointed to the hostages and laughed.

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:39.630
Others acted like tourists
taking in the sights,

00:17:39.630 --> 00:17:41.220
prompting one among us to joke

00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:44.160
that we were the main attraction
at the Jordanian branch

00:17:44.160 --> 00:17:45.183
of the Bronx Zoo.

00:17:46.800 --> 00:17:49.830
Within sight two were
tanks and heavy artillery,

00:17:49.830 --> 00:17:51.247
a reporter described, quote,

00:17:51.247 --> 00:17:54.150
"manned anti-aircraft and anti-tank guns"

00:17:54.150 --> 00:17:56.730
in trenches dug round the three aircraft

00:17:56.730 --> 00:17:58.560
and a big detachment of guerrillas

00:17:58.560 --> 00:18:00.000
armed with submachine guns

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:02.463
and hand grenades standing on the alert.

00:18:04.110 --> 00:18:07.080
We were surrounded in turn
by Jordanian army troops

00:18:07.080 --> 00:18:09.900
signaling our position in a war

00:18:09.900 --> 00:18:12.423
between Jordan and Palestinian insurgents.

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:15.420
Besides the guns and
grenades, and armaments,

00:18:15.420 --> 00:18:17.793
there was the dynamite
and a shifting deadline.

00:18:19.050 --> 00:18:21.120
A representative from the
International Red Cross

00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:24.060
came on board one day to
assure us that in his words,

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:26.820
the entire world knows
what's going on here

00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:29.490
and that everyone was
working on a solution.

00:18:29.490 --> 00:18:32.613
He then candidly added, we
don't know if we will succeed.

00:18:34.200 --> 00:18:36.120
Sometimes the commandos would tell us,

00:18:36.120 --> 00:18:39.330
we cannot wait forever and you
will all go to a fiery death

00:18:39.330 --> 00:18:41.013
if this thing isn't settled soon.

00:18:42.810 --> 00:18:46.380
Holocaust survivors among the
hostages face their own demons

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:50.250
with confinement and weaponry
stirring up unbidden memories

00:18:50.250 --> 00:18:53.610
only intensified by the
troublesome construction

00:18:53.610 --> 00:18:55.473
of Arabs as the new Nazis.

00:18:56.760 --> 00:18:59.490
One woman 38 years old
had gone to Bergen-Belsen

00:18:59.490 --> 00:19:00.690
in a cattle car.

00:19:00.690 --> 00:19:03.180
Her father was murdered
in Buchenwald and 10 aunts

00:19:03.180 --> 00:19:04.713
and uncles died in Auschwitz.

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:08.640
Her captivity inside the
hijacked plane brought me,

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:11.467
she would say later in her broken English,

00:19:11.467 --> 00:19:14.460
"All the memories again,
back from concentration camp,

00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:16.167
which was a very big nightmare."

00:19:18.540 --> 00:19:21.570
Writing the book I set out
to discover the contours

00:19:21.570 --> 00:19:23.310
of the demands of our captors,

00:19:23.310 --> 00:19:26.223
something I didn't entirely
understand at the time.

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:29.490
Sifting through communications preserved

00:19:29.490 --> 00:19:32.310
in the Richard Nixon Presidential
Library in California

00:19:32.310 --> 00:19:34.620
some marked "Top Secret."

00:19:34.620 --> 00:19:37.050
In the State Department
archives in Washington,

00:19:37.050 --> 00:19:38.490
in the Swiss Federal Archives

00:19:38.490 --> 00:19:40.680
and the British National Archives,

00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:43.110
I set out to make sense
of the negotiations

00:19:43.110 --> 00:19:45.543
for our release or its opposite.

00:19:47.340 --> 00:19:50.250
A document in the Nixon library
quoted a spokesman saying

00:19:50.250 --> 00:19:52.920
the Popular Front had
brought explosives on board,

00:19:52.920 --> 00:19:54.870
ready to quote, "destroy the planes"

00:19:54.870 --> 00:19:57.060
along with their prisoners
should the demands

00:19:57.060 --> 00:19:59.733
of the Popular Front not
be met by the deadline.

00:20:00.930 --> 00:20:03.210
I studied the document
with care to be sure

00:20:03.210 --> 00:20:06.000
I understood everything correctly.

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:09.120
The spokesman quote, "hedged
when asked if the passengers

00:20:09.120 --> 00:20:11.430
would be permitted to get off first,

00:20:11.430 --> 00:20:13.890
but gorilla sources said
the plan was apparently

00:20:13.890 --> 00:20:16.533
to blow up the hostages, also," unquote.

00:20:18.180 --> 00:20:21.870
The commando's ultimatum was
repeated in another telegram,

00:20:21.870 --> 00:20:24.840
quote, "Time period for
meeting all of the demands

00:20:24.840 --> 00:20:26.430
is 72 hours.

00:20:26.430 --> 00:20:28.650
If demands are not met within this time,

00:20:28.650 --> 00:20:31.377
planes with all passengers
will be blown up."

00:20:32.460 --> 00:20:35.940
A Swiss official described by
a colleague as level-headed

00:20:35.940 --> 00:20:38.340
and not given to emotional talk

00:20:38.340 --> 00:20:41.760
noted the serious possibility
of, quote, "a bloodbath"

00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:42.593
in the desert.

00:20:43.650 --> 00:20:46.800
The weapons and the
dynamite and the deadline,

00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:49.140
when I returned home I would say,

00:20:49.140 --> 00:20:50.790
I didn't really think about that.

00:20:52.680 --> 00:20:53.850
During my research,

00:20:53.850 --> 00:20:56.100
as I learned more about
my fellow hostages,

00:20:56.100 --> 00:20:58.890
I also discovered something else

00:20:58.890 --> 00:21:00.480
that my sister and I were different

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:03.423
from most of the other
American Jews on our plane.

00:21:05.240 --> 00:21:09.120
My father's parents were in
the words of his brother,

00:21:09.120 --> 00:21:11.853
world-class, non-practicing Jews.

00:21:12.840 --> 00:21:13.770
On my mother's side,

00:21:13.770 --> 00:21:15.660
my grandmother had refashioned herself

00:21:15.660 --> 00:21:19.380
from Libby Yetta to Lily Yvette.

00:21:19.380 --> 00:21:20.760
As part of that transformation,

00:21:20.760 --> 00:21:23.190
she spoke with an
effective British accent,

00:21:23.190 --> 00:21:26.520
prepared ham for Sunday
dinner and bought Easter hats

00:21:26.520 --> 00:21:27.670
for her granddaughters.

00:21:28.770 --> 00:21:30.330
Our reason for traveling to Israel

00:21:30.330 --> 00:21:33.000
also made my sister and me different.

00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:35.550
This was only three
years after the 1967 war

00:21:35.550 --> 00:21:37.380
when American Jews flocked to Israel,

00:21:37.380 --> 00:21:40.470
happily visiting the
territories rested from Jordan,

00:21:40.470 --> 00:21:41.760
the old city of Jerusalem,

00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:44.250
including the Western
wall and the holy sites

00:21:44.250 --> 00:21:47.880
of Hebrew and Jericho, and
Bethlehem on the West Bank.

00:21:47.880 --> 00:21:50.460
No one ever took Catherine
and me to those sites,

00:21:50.460 --> 00:21:52.350
and the only reason we
were going to Israel

00:21:52.350 --> 00:21:54.420
was to visit our mother.

00:21:54.420 --> 00:21:57.060
In the words of one dance historian,

00:21:57.060 --> 00:22:00.390
though of Jewish extraction,
Linda Hodes, my mother

00:22:00.390 --> 00:22:03.303
had never been interested
in Israel or Zionism.

00:22:04.590 --> 00:22:07.110
When my mother and Israeli
stepfather took my sister

00:22:07.110 --> 00:22:10.800
and me to visit friends in
the artist colony of Ein Hod,

00:22:10.800 --> 00:22:13.830
we had no idea that the
hundreds of Palestinians there

00:22:13.830 --> 00:22:17.730
in 1948 had been exiled to refugee camps,

00:22:17.730 --> 00:22:20.583
the village soon resettled
by Israeli artists.

00:22:21.990 --> 00:22:24.937
In the letter, "Home to our
Father," Catherine wrote,

00:22:24.937 --> 00:22:27.420
"Painters, printers,
and sculptors live there

00:22:27.420 --> 00:22:30.300
in quaint little Arab style houses,

00:22:30.300 --> 00:22:32.940
enraptured and entirely
unaware of the history

00:22:32.940 --> 00:22:34.770
she was describing.

00:22:34.770 --> 00:22:37.560
Everywhere are beautiful
mountain views and flowers,"

00:22:37.560 --> 00:22:38.393
she wrote.

00:22:39.720 --> 00:22:42.570
I had no idea that Palestinians
had once lived in Israel

00:22:42.570 --> 00:22:46.083
because many Jews in
1970 had no idea either.

00:22:47.670 --> 00:22:50.820
The historian, Omar Bartov,
whose elders made their way

00:22:50.820 --> 00:22:54.030
from eastern Europe to
Palestine lived, he wrote,

00:22:54.030 --> 00:22:58.050
next to quote-unquote,
"abandoned" Palestinian villages.

00:22:58.050 --> 00:23:00.000
But as a child, he never once,

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:03.183
those are his words, considered
the origins of those ruins,

00:23:04.500 --> 00:23:06.300
nor did the Israeli kids we played with

00:23:06.300 --> 00:23:09.120
have anything to teach us on this count.

00:23:09.120 --> 00:23:11.610
Israeli writer, Ari Shavit around my age,

00:23:11.610 --> 00:23:14.220
noted that his grandparents
and parents proceeded

00:23:14.220 --> 00:23:18.297
with their lives as if Palestinians
quote, "never existed."

00:23:20.190 --> 00:23:22.950
Catherine and I were aware
that our captors wanted us

00:23:22.950 --> 00:23:25.650
to empathize with them
and we were interested

00:23:25.650 --> 00:23:27.750
in the stories they told.

00:23:27.750 --> 00:23:29.790
This is how we live every day.

00:23:29.790 --> 00:23:31.620
This is all we have to eat.

00:23:31.620 --> 00:23:32.853
We have no homes.

00:23:34.290 --> 00:23:37.530
And once again said the
aviator in the Little Prince,

00:23:37.530 --> 00:23:41.463
without understanding why I
had a queer sense of sorrow.

00:23:42.960 --> 00:23:45.480
Children trying to make
sense of global politics

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:48.300
in human terms, Catherine
and I stirred the new history

00:23:48.300 --> 00:23:50.310
we had learned in the desert

00:23:50.310 --> 00:23:52.620
into the history we already knew

00:23:52.620 --> 00:23:55.710
and stirred the pain of our
captors into our own fears

00:23:55.710 --> 00:23:57.633
and the fears of our fellow hostages.

00:23:58.530 --> 00:24:01.080
In Israel, our friend's
parents had taken refuge

00:24:01.080 --> 00:24:03.690
from the Holocaust,
witnessed the death of family

00:24:03.690 --> 00:24:05.430
and the loss of homes in Europe,

00:24:05.430 --> 00:24:07.893
escaping the destruction
of the Jewish people.

00:24:08.850 --> 00:24:12.420
Our captors, who bore no
responsibility for the Holocaust,

00:24:12.420 --> 00:24:14.790
had in turn lost their homes in Palestine,

00:24:14.790 --> 00:24:17.640
many of them raised in refugee camps.

00:24:17.640 --> 00:24:18.780
History was the key,

00:24:18.780 --> 00:24:21.993
yet each side told completely
different histories.

00:24:23.557 --> 00:24:25.380
"What are your feelings
toward the guerrillas now?"

00:24:25.380 --> 00:24:27.330
My father would ask when we got home,

00:24:27.330 --> 00:24:30.097
and I have this conversation on tape.

00:24:30.097 --> 00:24:32.310
"The Palestinians,"
Catherine corrects him.

00:24:32.310 --> 00:24:34.830
No anger, no resentment.

00:24:34.830 --> 00:24:36.780
My voice comes on the tape.

00:24:36.780 --> 00:24:38.850
They have some type of
point which I didn't know

00:24:38.850 --> 00:24:40.597
before I offer.

00:24:40.597 --> 00:24:42.750
"They have something
they're really trying to say

00:24:42.750 --> 00:24:43.920
and they need to say it.

00:24:43.920 --> 00:24:45.810
It's pretty sad."

00:24:45.810 --> 00:24:47.880
My sister continues.

00:24:47.880 --> 00:24:50.370
I was very naive about
the political aspect.

00:24:50.370 --> 00:24:52.983
I learned more, a lot
more than I knew before.

00:24:53.910 --> 00:24:56.520
Their purpose was to get back
their home, she explains,

00:24:56.520 --> 00:24:59.100
and the plea of any people's
to get back their home

00:24:59.100 --> 00:25:00.183
is a valid plea.

00:25:01.380 --> 00:25:03.210
My sister and I puzzled out the question,

00:25:03.210 --> 00:25:06.720
could Israelis and Palestinians
live on the same land?

00:25:06.720 --> 00:25:09.210
Maybe it had once been
possible, we thought,

00:25:09.210 --> 00:25:10.680
but there was so much anger now,

00:25:10.680 --> 00:25:14.070
so much hatred that it
didn't seem possible anymore.

00:25:14.070 --> 00:25:15.670
We couldn't think of a solution.

00:25:16.837 --> 00:25:19.740
"I stopped in my tracks,
my heart torn asunder,

00:25:19.740 --> 00:25:22.620
but still I did not
understand," said the aviator

00:25:22.620 --> 00:25:24.120
in Little Prince.

00:25:24.120 --> 00:25:24.990
There in the desert,

00:25:24.990 --> 00:25:27.363
Catherine and I just
felt sorry for everyone.

00:25:29.190 --> 00:25:32.520
Now in part, the book traces
my journey toward understanding

00:25:32.520 --> 00:25:34.710
that my sister and I had really been there

00:25:34.710 --> 00:25:38.343
inside that plane in the
desert, held hostage for a week.

00:25:39.390 --> 00:25:41.490
In the National Archives in Washington,

00:25:41.490 --> 00:25:44.910
I watched raw news footage
preserved by the CIA

00:25:44.910 --> 00:25:46.470
of our release.

00:25:46.470 --> 00:25:48.540
Though at the time,
Catherine and I didn't know

00:25:48.540 --> 00:25:50.850
where we were going,
where we were being taken

00:25:50.850 --> 00:25:52.323
or what would happen next.

00:25:53.580 --> 00:25:56.670
It was early afternoon
when we arrived in a van

00:25:56.670 --> 00:25:59.613
at the Intercontinental
Hotel in Amman, Jordan.

00:26:00.690 --> 00:26:03.900
Watching the tapes, I see
some of what I remembered.

00:26:03.900 --> 00:26:07.890
Soldiers direct our vehicle,
a commando holds a megaphone.

00:26:07.890 --> 00:26:10.113
Horns honk, people speak in Arabic.

00:26:11.070 --> 00:26:14.700
13 minutes in the tape switches
to the inside of the van.

00:26:14.700 --> 00:26:17.250
Passengers displaying
expressionless caution.

00:26:17.250 --> 00:26:19.260
A photographer had thrust
his camera straight

00:26:19.260 --> 00:26:22.980
into one of the open
windows, and there we are,

00:26:22.980 --> 00:26:24.543
and I'll show you an image.

00:26:31.050 --> 00:26:32.553
Here's a still photograph.

00:26:34.080 --> 00:26:38.160
I sit impassively hand
to cheek, shielding me,

00:26:38.160 --> 00:26:41.880
partly obscuring me, Catherine
looks straight into the lens,

00:26:41.880 --> 00:26:44.403
serious, responsible, afraid.

00:26:45.930 --> 00:26:48.870
On the tape, she twists around
to look out the windows,

00:26:48.870 --> 00:26:51.510
scanning the commotion,
planning what to do,

00:26:51.510 --> 00:26:54.840
thinking how can I keep
us from being separated?

00:26:54.840 --> 00:26:56.553
How can I keep Martha safe?

00:26:57.630 --> 00:26:59.460
Tentatively then on the tape,

00:26:59.460 --> 00:27:01.500
I look around too.

00:27:01.500 --> 00:27:03.900
Right there on the screen before my eyes

00:27:03.900 --> 00:27:07.530
is the 12-year-old girl
I've been trying to conjure.

00:27:07.530 --> 00:27:09.750
But there's that feeling again.

00:27:09.750 --> 00:27:11.280
Despite all the documents,

00:27:11.280 --> 00:27:14.730
despite all the fellow hostages
I've met and talked to,

00:27:14.730 --> 00:27:17.733
many of whom after all don't
remember Catherine and me.

00:27:19.350 --> 00:27:22.080
There's the feeling that the
hijacking must have happened

00:27:22.080 --> 00:27:23.103
to someone else.

00:27:24.270 --> 00:27:26.430
Studying the moving image of that girl

00:27:26.430 --> 00:27:29.580
makes me feel more than
anything else like a historian

00:27:29.580 --> 00:27:31.860
coming upon a visual representation

00:27:31.860 --> 00:27:34.953
of her long research
subject for the first time.

00:27:36.090 --> 00:27:39.240
Watching the tape all those
years later I write down,

00:27:39.240 --> 00:27:40.863
I can't believe I was there.

00:27:44.310 --> 00:27:46.080
I'm going to dedicate this next passage

00:27:46.080 --> 00:27:48.750
about our arrival at
Kennedy Airport to my father

00:27:48.750 --> 00:27:50.460
who died at the age of 98,

00:27:50.460 --> 00:27:53.160
before he had a chance to read the book.

00:27:53.160 --> 00:27:55.050
Of course, I could have
given him the manuscript

00:27:55.050 --> 00:27:56.853
at any point, but I never did.

00:27:58.740 --> 00:28:01.140
I see my father before he sees us.

00:28:01.140 --> 00:28:02.910
He's so pale that the words white

00:28:02.910 --> 00:28:05.310
as a sheet leap into my mind.

00:28:05.310 --> 00:28:07.500
Then he sees us and bolts forward,

00:28:07.500 --> 00:28:09.240
and Catherine sees him too.

00:28:09.240 --> 00:28:11.880
His expression changing
instantly from grave

00:28:11.880 --> 00:28:15.120
to beaming now waving at us wildly.

00:28:15.120 --> 00:28:17.460
He breaks into a run and so do we.

00:28:17.460 --> 00:28:21.030
The scene I'd imagined so
many times coming to life.

00:28:21.030 --> 00:28:23.790
My father folds his arms around both of us

00:28:23.790 --> 00:28:25.297
at the same time.

00:28:25.297 --> 00:28:28.407
"Oh dad," Catherine says, "we
were so worried about you."

00:28:29.820 --> 00:28:32.430
My stepmother stands by astonished

00:28:32.430 --> 00:28:34.320
that the two of us are so calm.

00:28:34.320 --> 00:28:37.827
In her words, "no crying,
no tears, ready to go."

00:28:38.917 --> 00:28:40.920
"Nothing about him," said the aviator

00:28:40.920 --> 00:28:44.670
in the Little Prince gave any
suggestion of a child lost

00:28:44.670 --> 00:28:46.070
in the middle of the desert.

00:28:47.670 --> 00:28:50.287
Decades later researching
the book, I asked my father,

00:28:50.287 --> 00:28:52.417
"Did you ever think we were going to die?"

00:28:52.417 --> 00:28:53.797
"No," he says, right away,

00:28:53.797 --> 00:28:55.980
"I always knew you were coming back,

00:28:55.980 --> 00:28:58.833
not for a second." He tells
me, did he think otherwise?

00:28:59.790 --> 00:29:02.250
My stepmother remembers
it differently though.

00:29:02.250 --> 00:29:04.980
She describes my father
during those days and nights

00:29:04.980 --> 00:29:09.300
as a man on another planet
at one stoic, frozen,

00:29:09.300 --> 00:29:10.830
and devastated.

00:29:10.830 --> 00:29:13.260
Was he thinking of never
getting you back? She says.

00:29:13.260 --> 00:29:14.370
Well, yes.

00:29:14.370 --> 00:29:16.773
Killed and never coming back? Oh, yes.

00:29:18.750 --> 00:29:21.090
And the last bit I'll share
this afternoon takes place

00:29:21.090 --> 00:29:23.013
in the summer of 2019.

00:29:24.907 --> 00:29:27.450
"So this is your first
official visit to Jordan?"

00:29:27.450 --> 00:29:30.390
Says our guide, Salah Layfat
trim in his blue jeans

00:29:30.390 --> 00:29:32.583
and short sleeve green shirt.

00:29:33.600 --> 00:29:34.980
Though his degrees in economics,

00:29:34.980 --> 00:29:37.110
Salah has a special interest in travelers

00:29:37.110 --> 00:29:39.183
who care about history and politics.

00:29:40.110 --> 00:29:42.750
The makeshift desert
airstrip no longer exists

00:29:42.750 --> 00:29:44.220
now buried beneath the buildings

00:29:44.220 --> 00:29:46.323
of a recently constructed defense plant.

00:29:47.580 --> 00:29:49.770
At the security station
that blocks the entrance,

00:29:49.770 --> 00:29:52.260
men emerge, shake hands, speak in Arabic.

00:29:52.260 --> 00:29:54.780
I can see Salah gesturing
toward me explaining

00:29:54.780 --> 00:29:56.160
why I'm there.

00:29:56.160 --> 00:29:58.140
He comes back to the car
to say that the plant

00:29:58.140 --> 00:29:59.940
is a completely restricted area,

00:29:59.940 --> 00:30:01.690
that it's impossible to gain entry.

00:30:03.240 --> 00:30:05.460
The security men have
given Salah directions

00:30:05.460 --> 00:30:08.280
to an unrestricted high
point, and we head south,

00:30:08.280 --> 00:30:12.150
then west, our driver pulling
onto a narrow paved road,

00:30:12.150 --> 00:30:15.930
then a dirt road until we come
to a stop near a trash dump

00:30:15.930 --> 00:30:18.723
with feral dogs scrounging
in the detritus.

00:30:20.340 --> 00:30:22.770
It's 8:30 in the morning
and it's already hot

00:30:22.770 --> 00:30:24.780
as we crunch across the powdery lime

00:30:24.780 --> 00:30:26.943
that covers our shoes in white dust.

00:30:28.057 --> 00:30:30.540
"You were on your way back to
the place where you landed?"

00:30:30.540 --> 00:30:32.613
The aviator asked a little prince.

00:30:34.027 --> 00:30:38.100
"At your time in 1970, this
was just desert," Salah says.

00:30:38.100 --> 00:30:40.800
Almost 50 years later,
I stand on the ledge

00:30:40.800 --> 00:30:44.130
looking north over a
flat, expansive scrub,

00:30:44.130 --> 00:30:47.190
a steel factory and parts
of a petroleum refinery rise

00:30:47.190 --> 00:30:48.360
to our west.

00:30:48.360 --> 00:30:51.543
The newest part of the city of
Zarqa spreads out behind us.

00:30:52.447 --> 00:30:55.020
"The sand, I think would be
in that area," Salah says,

00:30:55.020 --> 00:30:57.693
pointing to a low ridge
and the hills farther on.

00:30:58.650 --> 00:31:00.840
Then you don't remember
said the little prince,

00:31:00.840 --> 00:31:02.523
this is not the exact spot.

00:31:04.020 --> 00:31:06.150
Comparing the coordinates
of the satellite image

00:31:06.150 --> 00:31:07.410
to a cell phone compass,

00:31:07.410 --> 00:31:09.900
Bruce, my husband with me that day,

00:31:09.900 --> 00:31:12.350
determines that we're less
than half a mile away.

00:31:13.260 --> 00:31:15.540
We're standing on the crest
where reporters coming

00:31:15.540 --> 00:31:17.760
from a Amman caught sight
of a three airplanes

00:31:17.760 --> 00:31:21.003
laid out like children's toys
or three dots on a domino.

00:31:22.080 --> 00:31:24.780
Envisioning Catherine and
me, two young hostages,

00:31:24.780 --> 00:31:27.633
Bruce loses his composure
behind his sunglasses.

00:31:28.620 --> 00:31:31.800
An occasional brush of wind
relieves the solid morning heat,

00:31:31.800 --> 00:31:34.027
which suddenly seems familiar.

00:31:34.027 --> 00:31:36.840
"I remember that it was
hot like this," I say.

00:31:36.840 --> 00:31:38.130
The airplane doors were open

00:31:38.130 --> 00:31:40.530
and sometimes the breeze would come in.

00:31:40.530 --> 00:31:42.980
Bruce is taking pictures
panning with his camera.

00:31:44.047 --> 00:31:46.620
"Take your time guys,
not to rush," Salah says.

00:31:46.620 --> 00:31:49.297
You can tag the photos with your location.

00:31:49.297 --> 00:31:51.300
"Yes, there'll be GPS tagged," Bruce says,

00:31:51.300 --> 00:31:52.980
steadying his voice.

00:31:52.980 --> 00:31:54.180
This is where the sun came up,

00:31:54.180 --> 00:31:56.103
he observes pointing to the east.

00:31:57.420 --> 00:31:59.167
After such a nightmare, Salah says,

00:31:59.167 --> 00:32:00.600
"To come back and see the place

00:32:00.600 --> 00:32:03.030
where these things happened is something."

00:32:03.030 --> 00:32:05.880
This is the advantage of
being a child at that time

00:32:05.880 --> 00:32:08.643
because you hadn't any
idea about what's going on.

00:32:10.230 --> 00:32:13.507
We stood on that ledge for a
little more than half an hour.

00:32:13.507 --> 00:32:16.680
"Look at it carefully, so that
you will be sure to recognize

00:32:16.680 --> 00:32:19.050
it in case you travel
someday to the desert."

00:32:19.050 --> 00:32:20.950
Said the aviator in the Little Prince.

00:32:22.080 --> 00:32:23.640
I'm ready to leave.

00:32:23.640 --> 00:32:25.170
The thought comes to me,

00:32:25.170 --> 00:32:27.870
I don't ever need to come back here again.

00:32:27.870 --> 00:32:31.500
I say, "It feels like it
happened to someone else."

00:32:31.500 --> 00:32:32.700
Thank you for listening.

00:32:34.740 --> 00:32:36.693
- Thank you, Martha.

00:32:37.620 --> 00:32:38.820
That was wonderful.

00:32:38.820 --> 00:32:42.248
And if I can just say, you know,

00:32:42.248 --> 00:32:45.720
I had heard Martha give
talks from the book

00:32:45.720 --> 00:32:46.950
and I had discussed it with her,

00:32:46.950 --> 00:32:51.090
and I really wasn't
prepared for how moving

00:32:51.090 --> 00:32:54.513
I found the book to be
as I read it this summer.

00:32:55.350 --> 00:32:58.290
You know, I knew it was gonna
be fascinating historically,

00:32:58.290 --> 00:33:02.280
but it's just an incredibly gripping read

00:33:02.280 --> 00:33:05.130
and it's also just very,
very moving for in ways

00:33:05.130 --> 00:33:08.010
that I'd like to try to get at today.

00:33:08.010 --> 00:33:11.130
And in fact, the first question,

00:33:11.130 --> 00:33:13.980
so I encourage everyone to
please send in questions

00:33:13.980 --> 00:33:17.670
using the Q&amp;A function at
the bottom of your screen.

00:33:17.670 --> 00:33:20.820
And we already have a couple questions,

00:33:20.820 --> 00:33:22.503
so I'm gonna dive right in.

00:33:23.610 --> 00:33:26.400
The first one, your eloquence and calmness

00:33:26.400 --> 00:33:29.730
in presenting the terrifying
events is striking to me.

00:33:29.730 --> 00:33:32.160
I imagine that the experience contributed

00:33:32.160 --> 00:33:34.560
to a kind of emotional distance.

00:33:34.560 --> 00:33:37.710
My question, did the
experience of writing the book

00:33:37.710 --> 00:33:40.860
bring more emotion to
your recall of the events?

00:33:40.860 --> 00:33:43.170
And that is having read the book,

00:33:43.170 --> 00:33:46.350
that is in a way what
you're after in a sense,

00:33:46.350 --> 00:33:47.500
but could you talk about that?

00:33:47.500 --> 00:33:49.020
- Yeah, it's such a wonderful question.

00:33:49.020 --> 00:33:49.853
Thank you.

00:33:52.110 --> 00:33:53.850
Writing the book was hard.

00:33:53.850 --> 00:33:55.443
It was hard for me emotionally.

00:33:56.280 --> 00:33:58.350
I was a fellow at the
Cullman Center for Scholars

00:33:58.350 --> 00:34:00.720
and Writers when I
began writing this book.

00:34:00.720 --> 00:34:04.320
And I had a wonderful support
of a cohort of fellows

00:34:04.320 --> 00:34:06.180
who are writing different kinds of works,

00:34:06.180 --> 00:34:08.940
nonfiction, fiction, history, poetry.

00:34:08.940 --> 00:34:12.570
And at the end of our fellowship year,

00:34:12.570 --> 00:34:14.013
I'll just share this story,

00:34:15.090 --> 00:34:17.280
one of the fellows who was
a very emotional person

00:34:17.280 --> 00:34:19.560
and who would often, you know,

00:34:19.560 --> 00:34:22.890
she would cry in the middle of
a conversation or at lunches.

00:34:22.890 --> 00:34:25.860
And so she joked at the
end of the fellowship year,

00:34:25.860 --> 00:34:27.510
I think I'm the fellow
who's cried the most

00:34:27.510 --> 00:34:28.380
during this year.

00:34:28.380 --> 00:34:29.497
And then I said to her,

00:34:29.497 --> 00:34:32.070
"Well, that's if you
don't count crying alone

00:34:32.070 --> 00:34:33.567
in our offices."

00:34:33.567 --> 00:34:36.213
So writing was very hard.

00:34:37.320 --> 00:34:38.820
Publishing the book was hard.

00:34:38.820 --> 00:34:40.380
It was also hard for my sister

00:34:40.380 --> 00:34:42.600
who was so incredibly
supportive and amazing

00:34:42.600 --> 00:34:45.050
during the whole process
and read the manuscript.

00:34:46.740 --> 00:34:49.803
Talking about it, it's so
interesting talking about it.

00:34:51.210 --> 00:34:55.170
I have to, in a way,
assume that kind of calm,

00:34:55.170 --> 00:34:57.450
I don't know if I'd call it detached,

00:34:57.450 --> 00:35:02.130
but I have to be the writer
talking to an audience.

00:35:02.130 --> 00:35:05.790
But I've also found that
since the book came out,

00:35:05.790 --> 00:35:10.710
I've experienced other
kinds of emotional troubles.

00:35:10.710 --> 00:35:12.420
And you know, we always think of memoirs

00:35:12.420 --> 00:35:16.530
as somehow solving past anxieties.

00:35:16.530 --> 00:35:19.380
And I almost found it to be the opposite.

00:35:19.380 --> 00:35:22.530
Writing the book was, I'm
really glad I wrote the book

00:35:22.530 --> 00:35:24.750
and I'm really glad I researched the book,

00:35:24.750 --> 00:35:28.380
and that my sister shared
the experience with me,

00:35:28.380 --> 00:35:31.470
but having the book come out
and having the book be out

00:35:31.470 --> 00:35:32.970
is actually quite difficult.

00:35:32.970 --> 00:35:37.970
So the questioner is correct
that my demeanor is, you know,

00:35:41.760 --> 00:35:46.760
something that I'm very
conscious of having to assume

00:35:50.430 --> 00:35:52.890
when I talk to groups and
talk to readers and people,

00:35:52.890 --> 00:35:55.470
which I very much want to do
and very much enjoy doing.

00:35:55.470 --> 00:35:57.333
So thank you for that question.

00:35:58.440 --> 00:36:00.243
- And another one,

00:36:01.200 --> 00:36:04.200
you said it felt like it
happened to someone else,

00:36:04.200 --> 00:36:06.450
and that's a striking sentence.

00:36:06.450 --> 00:36:09.270
As a historian, would
you say that you've tried

00:36:09.270 --> 00:36:13.740
to help people feel like
things happen to them?

00:36:13.740 --> 00:36:15.567
- Also, a wonderful question, thank you.

00:36:15.567 --> 00:36:17.130
And I say it's a wonderful question

00:36:17.130 --> 00:36:20.790
because I wanted to write a book

00:36:20.790 --> 00:36:25.290
that somebody could
read without having had

00:36:25.290 --> 00:36:28.740
the same kind of traumatic
experience I had.

00:36:28.740 --> 00:36:30.933
And that it could speak to people who,

00:36:32.010 --> 00:36:34.890
maybe especially people to
whom things had happened

00:36:34.890 --> 00:36:38.040
as children that they were not encouraged

00:36:38.040 --> 00:36:41.160
to talk about at the time,
things they didn't understand

00:36:41.160 --> 00:36:42.720
that happened to them as children.

00:36:42.720 --> 00:36:44.850
And now of course, we're grownups

00:36:44.850 --> 00:36:46.740
and we can go back and try to make sense

00:36:46.740 --> 00:36:49.020
of some of the things that happened to us.

00:36:49.020 --> 00:36:52.950
And what I found was in
a very gratifying way,

00:36:52.950 --> 00:36:55.800
I have received messages from readers

00:36:55.800 --> 00:37:00.420
who experienced very different
kinds of very sad events

00:37:00.420 --> 00:37:04.530
as young people, quite a few
from people who lost a parent

00:37:04.530 --> 00:37:08.460
as a young child and some in
very traumatic circumstances.

00:37:08.460 --> 00:37:11.250
One from a reader who
was a very young child

00:37:11.250 --> 00:37:14.010
in a car accident that killed her mother,

00:37:14.010 --> 00:37:15.300
but she was a survivor

00:37:15.300 --> 00:37:17.400
and nobody ever talked to her about it.

00:37:17.400 --> 00:37:20.760
I also received an incredibly,
incredibly moving message

00:37:20.760 --> 00:37:25.352
from somebody who had
been violently assaulted

00:37:25.352 --> 00:37:28.530
in an incident that he
named as gay bashing,

00:37:28.530 --> 00:37:30.930
he's in the hospital for four months

00:37:30.930 --> 00:37:33.570
and wrote in this message that
although, you know, he said,

00:37:33.570 --> 00:37:35.490
they fixed me up pretty well physically,

00:37:35.490 --> 00:37:37.710
he was still trying to
deal with it emotionally.

00:37:37.710 --> 00:37:39.311
And all of these messages said,

00:37:39.311 --> 00:37:42.150
you know, your book helped
me think about these things

00:37:42.150 --> 00:37:43.710
I didn't wanna think about before.

00:37:43.710 --> 00:37:45.390
Or some of them were from people who said,

00:37:45.390 --> 00:37:47.550
your book reminded me of the process

00:37:47.550 --> 00:37:49.140
that I went through when I, at first,

00:37:49.140 --> 00:37:51.930
didn't want to think about
things and then turn to them.

00:37:51.930 --> 00:37:55.290
And so that was really important to me

00:37:55.290 --> 00:37:58.770
that the book could transcend
its particular trauma

00:37:58.770 --> 00:38:01.890
and topic and speak to
readers in that way.

00:38:01.890 --> 00:38:03.000
- Yeah, that's what I mean.

00:38:03.000 --> 00:38:07.740
I lost a parent very young
and then very quickly decided

00:38:07.740 --> 00:38:10.830
that, you know, that was
something I had moved on

00:38:10.830 --> 00:38:13.800
and that that was the part
that really resonated for me,

00:38:13.800 --> 00:38:15.660
you know, that you went back to school,

00:38:15.660 --> 00:38:18.210
and acted like everything was okay,

00:38:18.210 --> 00:38:21.210
and I remembered something very similar.

00:38:21.210 --> 00:38:22.510
I'm wondering whether

00:38:23.910 --> 00:38:28.290
the kind of work on trauma
was important to you

00:38:28.290 --> 00:38:30.453
as you wrote this and specifically,

00:38:31.410 --> 00:38:36.410
the idea of kind of delayed
onset PTSD and you know,

00:38:36.900 --> 00:38:40.170
Freud talks about the
concept of Nachträglichkeit

00:38:40.170 --> 00:38:44.340
and a kind of, where, as I understand it,

00:38:44.340 --> 00:38:47.800
a subsequent event can trigger

00:38:48.990 --> 00:38:52.140
a feeling of trauma
related to an earlier event

00:38:52.140 --> 00:38:55.290
that you didn't necessarily
register as trauma at the time.

00:38:55.290 --> 00:38:59.040
And it made me think of
your experience of 9/11

00:38:59.040 --> 00:39:02.220
as that kind of second event here.

00:39:02.220 --> 00:39:04.860
I'm wondering if any of
that kind of trauma theory

00:39:04.860 --> 00:39:06.000
was important to you?

00:39:06.000 --> 00:39:08.850
- Yeah, so I have a big long bibliography

00:39:08.850 --> 00:39:12.300
in the back of the book and
readers who skimmed through it

00:39:12.300 --> 00:39:14.010
will see that I read many,

00:39:14.010 --> 00:39:18.450
many professional journal
articles from psychology

00:39:18.450 --> 00:39:20.280
and psychiatry journals about trauma.

00:39:20.280 --> 00:39:24.390
What was tough was that very few,

00:39:24.390 --> 00:39:27.690
really none of the articles were based on

00:39:27.690 --> 00:39:29.520
the kinds of experiments or circumstances

00:39:29.520 --> 00:39:31.590
that replicated our own.

00:39:31.590 --> 00:39:34.080
So articles about trauma and children

00:39:34.080 --> 00:39:39.080
are usually about sexual abuse
or witnessing an accident,

00:39:39.120 --> 00:39:42.240
something like, those
were two common phenomena

00:39:42.240 --> 00:39:44.086
that were often in these articles.

00:39:44.086 --> 00:39:48.570
There is one wonderful
writer and psychologist

00:39:48.570 --> 00:39:50.520
who wrote quite extensively about children

00:39:50.520 --> 00:39:52.560
who were on a hijacked school bus.

00:39:52.560 --> 00:39:53.820
And although it was very different,

00:39:53.820 --> 00:39:56.220
her work was very important to me.

00:39:56.220 --> 00:39:58.410
So that was incredibly important to me,

00:39:58.410 --> 00:39:59.640
and I did learn from it,

00:39:59.640 --> 00:40:01.800
but it's also important,
and I do write about this

00:40:01.800 --> 00:40:05.280
in the book that, you
know, PTSD was not named

00:40:05.280 --> 00:40:07.620
until 10 years after the hijacking.

00:40:07.620 --> 00:40:08.700
It was named in 1980.

00:40:08.700 --> 00:40:10.440
Now of course, it happened,

00:40:10.440 --> 00:40:12.150
and you know, I'm a Civil War historian,

00:40:12.150 --> 00:40:15.240
people talked about
soldiers and shellshock,

00:40:15.240 --> 00:40:18.090
and all of that kind of,
obviously, people were aware of it,

00:40:18.090 --> 00:40:19.650
but it wasn't named.

00:40:19.650 --> 00:40:23.010
And what I noticed in doing my research

00:40:23.010 --> 00:40:28.010
was that all kinds of authorities
spoke about the event,

00:40:28.380 --> 00:40:30.570
kind of like you were
saying a moment ago, Maury,

00:40:30.570 --> 00:40:33.570
about your mother as if
it's over and that's fine.

00:40:33.570 --> 00:40:36.480
And so I was astounded to
hear a newscaster just say

00:40:36.480 --> 00:40:38.460
the words, and now
their nightmare was over

00:40:38.460 --> 00:40:39.483
when we got home.

00:40:40.920 --> 00:40:45.920
I also found that I very much
took on that narrative myself.

00:40:46.170 --> 00:40:49.410
So one of my favorite
examples during my research

00:40:49.410 --> 00:40:53.400
was my best friend in
Israel was kind enough

00:40:53.400 --> 00:40:55.680
to share with me the letters
that she and I had written

00:40:55.680 --> 00:40:57.930
to each other after we got home.

00:40:57.930 --> 00:41:00.090
And in one of them I wrote to her,

00:41:00.090 --> 00:41:02.880
it was the first letter I
wrote to her after I got home.

00:41:02.880 --> 00:41:05.310
And I, you know, wrote her a few things,

00:41:05.310 --> 00:41:06.570
didn't say anything about the hijacking.

00:41:06.570 --> 00:41:08.700
And then I said, I'll
write you a longer letter

00:41:08.700 --> 00:41:10.620
when more things have happened, you know,

00:41:10.620 --> 00:41:12.093
as if nothing had happened.

00:41:13.440 --> 00:41:16.170
And so I also talked to friends of mine,

00:41:16.170 --> 00:41:17.220
I was entering the seventh grade

00:41:17.220 --> 00:41:19.260
who I talked to friends
of mine in seventh grade

00:41:19.260 --> 00:41:20.093
what their memories were?

00:41:20.093 --> 00:41:22.650
And one of them said
very, very poignantly,

00:41:22.650 --> 00:41:24.960
she said to me, the
impression that the girls

00:41:24.960 --> 00:41:26.977
in your class had was,
these are her words,

00:41:26.977 --> 00:41:29.700
"no harm done, no lasting effect."

00:41:29.700 --> 00:41:33.780
So I was very determined not to absorb

00:41:33.780 --> 00:41:34.800
what had happened to me.

00:41:34.800 --> 00:41:37.920
And that was the way for me to prove,

00:41:37.920 --> 00:41:42.240
I believe now to prove its
insignificance to myself.

00:41:42.240 --> 00:41:44.670
And part of it was about
protecting my parents.

00:41:44.670 --> 00:41:46.650
I didn't want them to
know that I had suffered

00:41:46.650 --> 00:41:48.570
because I knew that they had suffered.

00:41:48.570 --> 00:41:50.460
So, you know, this is
the challenge of writing

00:41:50.460 --> 00:41:53.130
about childhood trauma
and childhood memories,

00:41:53.130 --> 00:41:55.110
and all of that was part of the process

00:41:55.110 --> 00:41:57.510
of researching the book, writing the book,

00:41:57.510 --> 00:42:01.410
and then also thinking about
the book since its publication.

00:42:01.410 --> 00:42:04.320
- Okay, we have quite
a lot of questions now.

00:42:04.320 --> 00:42:09.320
So one, so you talk about how
Holocaust survivors experience

00:42:10.380 --> 00:42:12.090
the hijacking.

00:42:12.090 --> 00:42:15.540
Do you make the distinction
about being a young Jew

00:42:15.540 --> 00:42:19.140
having this experience versus
elderly people repeating

00:42:19.140 --> 00:42:21.930
a trauma that was particularly Jewish?

00:42:21.930 --> 00:42:24.001
- Yeah, that's a really
interesting question.

00:42:24.001 --> 00:42:26.880
I wouldn't say for sure.

00:42:26.880 --> 00:42:28.650
I mean, you can read the
book in different ways.

00:42:28.650 --> 00:42:30.093
I think in my reading,

00:42:30.990 --> 00:42:32.670
the only way I make that distinction

00:42:32.670 --> 00:42:34.680
is by saying that my sister and I knew

00:42:34.680 --> 00:42:37.350
there were Holocaust
survivors on the plane

00:42:37.350 --> 00:42:40.200
and they had often been
children during the Holocaust.

00:42:40.200 --> 00:42:41.460
We didn't know them well,

00:42:41.460 --> 00:42:42.690
but we knew that was true

00:42:42.690 --> 00:42:44.760
because we heard people talking about it

00:42:44.760 --> 00:42:47.910
and that we felt very sorry for them.

00:42:47.910 --> 00:42:50.850
Now, as I said in my talk,

00:42:50.850 --> 00:42:54.300
we also felt sorry for our
captors who had been children

00:42:54.300 --> 00:42:56.670
when their families had lost their homes.

00:42:56.670 --> 00:42:58.413
And so in a way,

00:42:59.509 --> 00:43:02.190
and I think I said the line from the book,

00:43:02.190 --> 00:43:04.590
we felt sorry for everyone.

00:43:04.590 --> 00:43:07.020
And of course, I did talk about, you know,

00:43:07.020 --> 00:43:09.000
that my sister and I were
different from other American Jews

00:43:09.000 --> 00:43:09.833
on the plane.

00:43:09.833 --> 00:43:11.760
So in a way I was 12, my sister was 13,

00:43:11.760 --> 00:43:14.614
we were trying to make
sense of all of this.

00:43:14.614 --> 00:43:17.730
I think we were empathic
children in our own ways.

00:43:17.730 --> 00:43:22.650
And so I don't believe
I made a distinction

00:43:22.650 --> 00:43:24.000
in the way the questioner asked

00:43:24.000 --> 00:43:26.130
and it's a very interesting distinction.

00:43:26.130 --> 00:43:28.830
It was more a matter
of trying to understand

00:43:28.830 --> 00:43:32.010
the circumstances of
different people in our midst,

00:43:32.010 --> 00:43:33.600
captors and captives.

00:43:33.600 --> 00:43:37.620
And again, kind of wanting to
solve everybody's problems,

00:43:37.620 --> 00:43:39.687
but not being able to
think of a way to do that.

00:43:39.687 --> 00:43:40.950
And the last thing I'll say,

00:43:40.950 --> 00:43:42.570
and I'll try to keep my responses short

00:43:42.570 --> 00:43:45.150
because there are many questions, but...

00:43:45.150 --> 00:43:46.080
Oh, I'm sorry, nevermind.

00:43:46.080 --> 00:43:47.370
I don't even remember
what I was gonna say.

00:43:47.370 --> 00:43:50.070
- Yeah, I mean, I do feel
like there are those moments

00:43:50.070 --> 00:43:51.723
in the book where, you know,

00:43:53.790 --> 00:43:56.040
I don't think this is my own
projection of the reading

00:43:56.040 --> 00:43:58.470
where you're talking about,
especially in the part,

00:43:58.470 --> 00:44:03.470
the, you know, where they ask
you whether you're Jewish,

00:44:03.750 --> 00:44:06.360
the hijackers, and that did,

00:44:06.360 --> 00:44:08.070
I think, I'm not imagining this,

00:44:08.070 --> 00:44:11.010
that you bring up that in
relation to the holocaust

00:44:11.010 --> 00:44:12.780
in the book.
- Yeah,

00:44:12.780 --> 00:44:14.610
so I'd like to just say
a little bit about that.

00:44:14.610 --> 00:44:15.813
It's very important.

00:44:16.680 --> 00:44:20.190
There was the second night we're
on the plane, Monday night,

00:44:20.190 --> 00:44:23.070
that was the one time where
the commandos questioned

00:44:23.070 --> 00:44:25.830
the hostages and asked each
hostage if they were Jewish.

00:44:25.830 --> 00:44:28.710
And my sister answered
for us, and she said yes.

00:44:28.710 --> 00:44:30.780
And then what happened that
night was quite frightening,

00:44:30.780 --> 00:44:33.240
was the most frightening
moment of the whole hijacking

00:44:33.240 --> 00:44:37.140
where, you know, people were
taken outside to the desert.

00:44:37.140 --> 00:44:38.760
It was nighttime and you know,

00:44:38.760 --> 00:44:40.827
there were armed commandos around us.

00:44:40.827 --> 00:44:43.320
And then what ultimately happened was

00:44:43.320 --> 00:44:46.380
a fair number of hostages were released.

00:44:46.380 --> 00:44:49.260
From our plane, the only
people who were released

00:44:49.260 --> 00:44:50.403
were not Jewish.

00:44:52.230 --> 00:44:56.400
The fact is the Jews
whose names were called

00:44:56.400 --> 00:44:58.200
were sent back to the plane,

00:44:58.200 --> 00:44:59.520
but very important,

00:44:59.520 --> 00:45:01.710
and I discovered this during my research,

00:45:01.710 --> 00:45:05.280
there were still many, many
non-Jews who were on the plane

00:45:05.280 --> 00:45:07.530
and who were retained the whole week.

00:45:07.530 --> 00:45:11.970
And our captors also kept
about 50 hostages longer

00:45:11.970 --> 00:45:13.290
for two weeks longer.

00:45:13.290 --> 00:45:17.160
And about two thirds of
those people were not Jewish.

00:45:17.160 --> 00:45:21.123
And also the same night
that some of the non-Jews

00:45:21.123 --> 00:45:24.960
from our plane, the TWA
plane were sent on to Amman,

00:45:24.960 --> 00:45:29.040
Jews from the Swiss
airplane were released.

00:45:29.040 --> 00:45:31.950
And that Swiss airplane had
not made a stop over in Israel.

00:45:31.950 --> 00:45:34.500
And people surmise that
might have been why.

00:45:34.500 --> 00:45:36.720
So what I write about in the book

00:45:36.720 --> 00:45:39.030
is one of the enduring
myths of this hijacking

00:45:39.030 --> 00:45:42.150
is that our captors
separated Jews from non-Jews,

00:45:42.150 --> 00:45:44.160
held all the Jews and
let all the non-Jews go.

00:45:44.160 --> 00:45:45.723
And that's simply not true.

00:45:47.010 --> 00:45:50.760
But I do concede that it
was a frightening moment

00:45:50.760 --> 00:45:53.160
on Monday night and a confusing moment

00:45:53.160 --> 00:45:56.770
and that's, I think why that myth endured.

00:45:59.040 --> 00:46:03.090
You know, I also should
say that after that night,

00:46:03.090 --> 00:46:05.310
and, you know, this
isn't just my analysis,

00:46:05.310 --> 00:46:06.450
but this is from my research,

00:46:06.450 --> 00:46:08.520
there was never any
distinction made between Jews

00:46:08.520 --> 00:46:09.353
and non-Jews.

00:46:10.290 --> 00:46:11.760
Our captors did make a distinction

00:46:11.760 --> 00:46:14.070
between Jews and Zionists,

00:46:14.070 --> 00:46:15.960
and they were very clear about that.

00:46:15.960 --> 00:46:17.880
And occasionally when they
would interrogate people,

00:46:17.880 --> 00:46:21.450
they would ask them about
their views of Zionism

00:46:21.450 --> 00:46:22.950
because that was a way,

00:46:22.950 --> 00:46:24.090
so what they were trying to do

00:46:24.090 --> 00:46:26.130
when they were asking these
questions was to determine

00:46:26.130 --> 00:46:28.680
who would be a valuable
hostage to hold longer

00:46:28.680 --> 00:46:31.110
because they wanted to
exchange their hostages

00:46:31.110 --> 00:46:32.700
for Palestinian prisoners.

00:46:32.700 --> 00:46:36.573
So a complicated, complex situation.

00:46:37.680 --> 00:46:40.410
And I feel strongly, and I
do set this out in the book,

00:46:40.410 --> 00:46:43.113
that all the facets of
it need to be understood.

00:46:44.250 --> 00:46:46.890
- And they also, as I remember,

00:46:46.890 --> 00:46:50.190
were very interested in certain Americans,

00:46:50.190 --> 00:46:51.510
especially on your plane,

00:46:51.510 --> 00:46:55.140
there was an African American
like who was in the army maybe

00:46:55.140 --> 00:46:57.510
who was kept the whole time.

00:46:57.510 --> 00:47:01.560
And there were two maybe people...

00:47:01.560 --> 00:47:02.520
Anyway, yeah, you-

00:47:02.520 --> 00:47:05.850
- Yeah, so some of the other
hostages you're referring to

00:47:05.850 --> 00:47:08.040
an African American soldier,
a man named Leonard Kane,

00:47:08.040 --> 00:47:10.200
who was kept longer.

00:47:10.200 --> 00:47:11.640
And also, I think you're
probably referring

00:47:11.640 --> 00:47:13.500
to there were two
diplomats, either of them

00:47:13.500 --> 00:47:14.430
were Jewish.
- Who eats,

00:47:14.430 --> 00:47:16.590
one of them eats like a secret message.

00:47:16.590 --> 00:47:19.020
- Yeah, so there was, I
didn't know this at the time,

00:47:19.020 --> 00:47:22.137
but what I learned was up in
the air while the hijacking

00:47:22.137 --> 00:47:25.500
was taking place, these were
two state department officials

00:47:25.500 --> 00:47:30.360
and one of them had his
notes, some notes with him.

00:47:30.360 --> 00:47:32.520
And you know, just as a
kind of diplomat sphere,

00:47:32.520 --> 00:47:34.662
he ended up ripping
them into little pieces

00:47:34.662 --> 00:47:36.240
and swallowing them.

00:47:36.240 --> 00:47:38.550
And, you know, I read that in one source

00:47:38.550 --> 00:47:40.020
and then it was corroborated

00:47:40.020 --> 00:47:43.320
because historians always
wanna corroborate stories.

00:47:43.320 --> 00:47:45.660
In another source in which a woman told me

00:47:45.660 --> 00:47:49.747
that her eight-year-old
son said her up in the air,

00:47:49.747 --> 00:47:52.680
"Mom, why are those men
behind us eating paper?"

00:47:52.680 --> 00:47:54.480
So I knew that that had happened.

00:47:54.480 --> 00:47:58.203
So people were kept for different reasons.

00:47:59.490 --> 00:48:03.750
The hijackers were also interested
in Americans on our plane

00:48:03.750 --> 00:48:05.580
who had dual Israeli citizenship.

00:48:05.580 --> 00:48:06.900
And there were actually very few of those.

00:48:06.900 --> 00:48:09.420
And one of the reasons
they were so interested

00:48:09.420 --> 00:48:12.240
was because one of the
other hijackings that day

00:48:12.240 --> 00:48:15.240
had been an El Al plane that
did take off from Israel.

00:48:15.240 --> 00:48:18.600
And there were of course,
many Israelis on that plane.

00:48:18.600 --> 00:48:21.660
And there was a real
dearth of Israeli citizens

00:48:21.660 --> 00:48:24.747
that they could leverage to
trade for Palestinian prisoners.

00:48:24.747 --> 00:48:27.990
And so they trained their
sites on dual citizens,

00:48:27.990 --> 00:48:30.660
of which there were actually
very few on our plane.

00:48:30.660 --> 00:48:35.160
- And you write about how
your sister answered for you

00:48:35.160 --> 00:48:39.870
when they asked if you were
Jewish and she decided to say

00:48:39.870 --> 00:48:40.980
that you were Jewish,

00:48:40.980 --> 00:48:43.830
but she didn't totally tell the truth

00:48:43.830 --> 00:48:47.640
that you were visiting your
mother who lived in Israel,

00:48:47.640 --> 00:48:49.020
right?
- Yes, that's exactly right.

00:48:49.020 --> 00:48:50.670
So she said we were Jewish.

00:48:50.670 --> 00:48:51.990
And then one of the questions

00:48:51.990 --> 00:48:55.200
that they also asked each
hostage that evening was,

00:48:55.200 --> 00:48:56.730
why were you in Israel?

00:48:56.730 --> 00:48:58.380
And what my sister said was,

00:48:58.380 --> 00:48:59.730
we are visiting our grandparents.

00:48:59.730 --> 00:49:01.920
Now, my grandparents did
live in Israel that summer,

00:49:01.920 --> 00:49:03.180
and that was true,

00:49:03.180 --> 00:49:07.260
but something stopped her from bringing up

00:49:07.260 --> 00:49:08.460
that our mother lived there.

00:49:08.460 --> 00:49:11.070
It felt too close and a
little too frightening to her.

00:49:11.070 --> 00:49:13.440
And I think she felt, you know,

00:49:13.440 --> 00:49:16.320
she had no intention about
lying about being Jewish,

00:49:16.320 --> 00:49:19.920
but she wasn't lying to
say that we were visiting

00:49:19.920 --> 00:49:22.620
our grandparents, she just
wasn't telling the whole story.

00:49:22.620 --> 00:49:24.060
- And that might have been smart

00:49:24.060 --> 00:49:25.470
because then they might have assumed

00:49:25.470 --> 00:49:27.945
that you were dual nationals in some way-

00:49:27.945 --> 00:49:28.778
- They might have.

00:49:28.778 --> 00:49:30.540
I mean, it's interesting
you're quite right.

00:49:30.540 --> 00:49:33.090
The fact is, and I do write
about this in the book,

00:49:34.470 --> 00:49:39.060
our captors were quite kind
to the children on the plane.

00:49:39.060 --> 00:49:41.430
That was not the experience
of all of the grownups

00:49:41.430 --> 00:49:43.950
by any means, especially the
ones who were interrogated,

00:49:43.950 --> 00:49:46.590
which happened by the way,
at night when we were asleep.

00:49:46.590 --> 00:49:48.210
And I had no idea this had happened

00:49:48.210 --> 00:49:50.670
until I was researching later.

00:49:50.670 --> 00:49:53.970
But they let us outside
for air and exercise,

00:49:53.970 --> 00:49:56.400
they jumped rope with us,
they sang songs with us.

00:49:56.400 --> 00:49:58.050
They gave little kids piggyback rides.

00:49:58.050 --> 00:49:59.190
You know, we're in the
middle of the desert,

00:49:59.190 --> 00:50:01.350
we can see water on the horizon

00:50:01.350 --> 00:50:02.820
'cause it was a mirage.

00:50:02.820 --> 00:50:06.120
And I had just learned about
mirage's in sixth grade,

00:50:06.120 --> 00:50:08.460
and one of the commandos, you
know, knelt down on the sand

00:50:08.460 --> 00:50:10.260
and explained the scientific
workings of mirages

00:50:10.260 --> 00:50:11.820
to a group of children.

00:50:11.820 --> 00:50:16.820
So they were not personally
frightening to us as children.

00:50:18.750 --> 00:50:20.370
- Yeah, so this, you've answered,

00:50:20.370 --> 00:50:21.780
some people have asked questions

00:50:21.780 --> 00:50:24.210
about whether your
recollections were different

00:50:24.210 --> 00:50:27.570
from the other passengers,
I think that answers.

00:50:27.570 --> 00:50:29.670
Here's an interesting question.

00:50:29.670 --> 00:50:32.790
You speak sensitively about
the events that influenced

00:50:32.790 --> 00:50:34.890
the hijackers political views.

00:50:34.890 --> 00:50:37.980
In what ways do you think
your experience helped shape

00:50:37.980 --> 00:50:40.020
what I take to be your political views

00:50:40.020 --> 00:50:42.690
of the Israeli-Palestinian conflict?

00:50:42.690 --> 00:50:44.250
- Yeah, it's such an interesting question.

00:50:44.250 --> 00:50:46.620
You know, don't forget
that I never thought

00:50:46.620 --> 00:50:50.583
about the hijacking for
years and years, and years.

00:50:53.400 --> 00:50:56.040
When I was researching, I mean,

00:50:56.040 --> 00:51:00.270
I knew obviously, I was
up on current events

00:51:00.270 --> 00:51:04.500
in terms of Israel-Palestine,
I would say my sister,

00:51:04.500 --> 00:51:06.360
who was only a year and
a half older than I,

00:51:06.360 --> 00:51:08.280
and just so smart and so savvy.

00:51:08.280 --> 00:51:10.470
I feel like she...

00:51:10.470 --> 00:51:12.360
So the thing about my
sister, which is so amazing,

00:51:12.360 --> 00:51:17.360
and this is in the book,
is she was so keen to be,

00:51:18.090 --> 00:51:20.640
to never to deny how
incredibly frightening

00:51:20.640 --> 00:51:22.140
and traumatic this experience was.

00:51:22.140 --> 00:51:25.050
She would never deny that
I was the one denying that.

00:51:25.050 --> 00:51:28.170
She also was simultaneously
able to hold in her mind

00:51:28.170 --> 00:51:31.860
and in her heart, this incredible
empathy for our captors.

00:51:31.860 --> 00:51:34.860
And she does that in a way that's unusual

00:51:34.860 --> 00:51:37.140
and very human and very admirable.

00:51:37.140 --> 00:51:40.890
And I think in some ways, I did that too,

00:51:40.890 --> 00:51:45.360
but I did it by
suppressing much more ideas

00:51:45.360 --> 00:51:48.090
about my feelings about fear.

00:51:48.090 --> 00:51:50.250
And then when I, you know,
really began to research

00:51:50.250 --> 00:51:52.080
the Popular Front, I
mean, look, first of all,

00:51:52.080 --> 00:51:55.140
you know, the Popular Front
was a revolutionary minority

00:51:55.140 --> 00:51:57.840
within the PLO in 1970.

00:51:57.840 --> 00:52:01.380
And the rest of the members of the PLO,

00:52:01.380 --> 00:52:04.170
which made up of many
factions in many groups,

00:52:04.170 --> 00:52:06.750
did not support the tactic
and strategy of hijacking.

00:52:06.750 --> 00:52:09.330
And they were quite
infuriated with the PFLP.

00:52:09.330 --> 00:52:12.190
So we can't take the
PFLP as representative

00:52:13.140 --> 00:52:15.240
of Palestinian views at the time,

00:52:15.240 --> 00:52:17.070
they were looked on with disfavor

00:52:17.070 --> 00:52:19.863
by fellow Palestinian insurgents.

00:52:20.880 --> 00:52:23.160
Nonetheless, you know,
researching all of that

00:52:23.160 --> 00:52:24.543
and all of that context,

00:52:25.860 --> 00:52:29.100
I think it clarified for
me some of my own views

00:52:29.100 --> 00:52:31.710
about the situation in the Middle East,

00:52:31.710 --> 00:52:34.350
which as a historian,

00:52:34.350 --> 00:52:36.750
the last thing I'll say,
you know, historians,

00:52:36.750 --> 00:52:39.900
we thrive on complexity and you know,

00:52:39.900 --> 00:52:42.600
the trouble with many dialogues
about Israel-Palestine

00:52:42.600 --> 00:52:45.720
is that neither side wants
to face the complexity.

00:52:45.720 --> 00:52:48.270
And so I try to do that in the book,

00:52:48.270 --> 00:52:51.570
and maybe the fact that
people on both sides

00:52:51.570 --> 00:52:53.700
have been disappointed with
the book in certain ways,

00:52:53.700 --> 00:52:56.640
or maybe I should say the
extremes of both sides.

00:52:56.640 --> 00:52:59.580
People have been disappointed
that I wasn't straight out

00:52:59.580 --> 00:53:02.850
pro-Israel and, you know,
in all of my renderings

00:53:02.850 --> 00:53:05.220
of this event and others were disappointed

00:53:05.220 --> 00:53:09.153
that I wasn't more sympathetic
to the Palestinian plight.

00:53:10.560 --> 00:53:12.390
I don't think it's that
I'm not taking a stand,

00:53:12.390 --> 00:53:15.600
but I think it's that
I'm trying to present

00:53:15.600 --> 00:53:18.250
the nuance and the complexity,
and that's hard to do.

00:53:20.130 --> 00:53:21.390
- So someone writes,

00:53:21.390 --> 00:53:23.250
both you and your sisters seem to have had

00:53:23.250 --> 00:53:26.340
a dispassionately mature
response to the hijacking

00:53:26.340 --> 00:53:29.250
at the time you were
considering the plight

00:53:29.250 --> 00:53:34.250
of the hijackers, did you later experience

00:53:36.990 --> 00:53:40.290
PTSD in the sense and has,
I think I know the answer

00:53:40.290 --> 00:53:43.170
to this, has the experience
altered your views

00:53:43.170 --> 00:53:47.550
on terrorism or hijacking,
you know, generally?

00:53:47.550 --> 00:53:50.670
- Yeah, I mean, so, you
know, PTSD wasn't named,

00:53:50.670 --> 00:53:52.020
but it doesn't mean it didn't exist.

00:53:52.020 --> 00:53:54.510
And so I read about the symptoms

00:53:54.510 --> 00:53:56.370
and I read about these conditions.

00:53:56.370 --> 00:53:58.470
My sister and I would
not have been diagnosed

00:53:58.470 --> 00:54:00.060
with PTSD when we came home.

00:54:00.060 --> 00:54:01.470
We would've been diagnosed with something

00:54:01.470 --> 00:54:03.240
called acute stress disorder,

00:54:03.240 --> 00:54:04.650
which is much more short-lived.

00:54:04.650 --> 00:54:07.890
So we did, we had bad dreams and you know,

00:54:07.890 --> 00:54:10.650
we had certain symptoms that
absolutely did affect us,

00:54:10.650 --> 00:54:13.650
but some people would have been,

00:54:13.650 --> 00:54:15.540
many people would've
been diagnosed with PTSD,

00:54:15.540 --> 00:54:17.190
but not everybody.

00:54:17.190 --> 00:54:19.410
So that's the first thing.

00:54:19.410 --> 00:54:22.080
I should also say that I had

00:54:22.080 --> 00:54:26.430
and still have, despite
researching and writing this book,

00:54:26.430 --> 00:54:28.950
flying on airplanes,
which I do very frequently

00:54:28.950 --> 00:54:32.010
is still very difficult for me.

00:54:32.010 --> 00:54:35.460
And, you know, still
engenders a lot of anxiety.

00:54:35.460 --> 00:54:37.500
So I completely willing to admit that.

00:54:37.500 --> 00:54:39.630
And tell me the second
part of the question again.

00:54:39.630 --> 00:54:43.805
- Yeah, did it change your view
of terrorism and hijacking?

00:54:43.805 --> 00:54:45.660
- No, I mean-
- It seems like 9/11

00:54:45.660 --> 00:54:49.533
is a motivating factor of
your writing the book, right?

00:54:50.940 --> 00:54:53.580
- Yes, I mean, it's so interesting

00:54:53.580 --> 00:54:57.810
because the word terrorism
is a complicated word.

00:54:57.810 --> 00:54:59.580
And I'm just gonna say a word about this

00:54:59.580 --> 00:55:01.410
because I am historian,

00:55:01.410 --> 00:55:04.110
I care about the historical
use of that word.

00:55:04.110 --> 00:55:08.130
And you know, experts disagree
on the use of that word.

00:55:08.130 --> 00:55:11.190
And historians puzzle out
the history of the word.

00:55:11.190 --> 00:55:14.190
And I was very interested
to find that in 1970,

00:55:14.190 --> 00:55:16.380
the mainstream press, so
like the New York Times

00:55:16.380 --> 00:55:20.070
did not use the word
terrorists much at all.

00:55:20.070 --> 00:55:21.630
So just-
- They used guerrillas, right?

00:55:21.630 --> 00:55:22.463
Didn't they call-
- Guerrillas,

00:55:22.463 --> 00:55:24.090
and so the words I use in the book,

00:55:24.090 --> 00:55:27.480
I do use commandos, guerrillas, captors,

00:55:27.480 --> 00:55:29.160
I use all of those words.

00:55:29.160 --> 00:55:31.410
Just to give one example,
there's a New York Times headline

00:55:31.410 --> 00:55:33.907
from September 10th, 1970 that reads,

00:55:33.907 --> 00:55:37.140
"Popular Front, most
militant commando group."

00:55:37.140 --> 00:55:38.460
So, you know, and that was,

00:55:38.460 --> 00:55:41.760
I got the word commando
actually not from my captors,

00:55:41.760 --> 00:55:43.260
but from the press.

00:55:43.260 --> 00:55:45.960
And I wanted to be historically accurate.

00:55:45.960 --> 00:55:47.910
And you know, people have written about

00:55:47.910 --> 00:55:50.640
the trajectory of the word terrorism,

00:55:50.640 --> 00:55:52.830
you know, the hard part
about the word terrorism

00:55:52.830 --> 00:55:57.830
is for whom do you use it and
for whom do you not use it?

00:55:58.110 --> 00:56:01.980
And so, you know, the leader and founder

00:56:01.980 --> 00:56:03.780
of the Popular Front
Liberation of Palestine,

00:56:03.780 --> 00:56:06.693
George Habash, who by
the way, was Christian,

00:56:07.950 --> 00:56:09.840
was not Muslim.

00:56:09.840 --> 00:56:12.207
You know, he would say that terrorism was,

00:56:12.207 --> 00:56:15.270
and these are his words, "was
what made of us refugees."

00:56:15.270 --> 00:56:17.013
So it's a complicated term.

00:56:18.510 --> 00:56:22.920
I mean, obviously, I didn't condone 9/11

00:56:22.920 --> 00:56:25.650
or the Munich Olympics,
or what happened at Tybee,

00:56:25.650 --> 00:56:27.303
or any of those things.

00:56:28.200 --> 00:56:29.670
And you know, even when we came home

00:56:29.670 --> 00:56:31.260
and I have this conversation on tape,

00:56:31.260 --> 00:56:33.090
and somebody asked my father, you know,

00:56:33.090 --> 00:56:34.470
how do you feel about the guerrillas,

00:56:34.470 --> 00:56:36.330
I think is what the questioner said.

00:56:36.330 --> 00:56:38.790
And my father said, "You know,
sympathetic to their cause,

00:56:38.790 --> 00:56:41.610
but not to their strategy and tactics."

00:56:41.610 --> 00:56:43.770
And so that also I think
was meaningful to me

00:56:43.770 --> 00:56:46.950
that my father could say
those words at the time.

00:56:46.950 --> 00:56:48.510
- Okay, we're almost out of time.

00:56:48.510 --> 00:56:50.880
I'm gonna take the privilege of asking you

00:56:50.880 --> 00:56:53.130
the last question myself,

00:56:53.130 --> 00:56:56.250
which is a very interesting
part in the book

00:56:56.250 --> 00:57:00.450
where you cite the hijackers saying

00:57:00.450 --> 00:57:02.850
that they were anti-Zionist,

00:57:02.850 --> 00:57:06.513
but not anti-Semitic or
anti-Jewish, I think.

00:57:08.070 --> 00:57:12.780
And you know, this of course,
is one of the main questions

00:57:12.780 --> 00:57:15.150
facing anti-Semitism studies now,

00:57:15.150 --> 00:57:19.320
when does anti-Zionism
turn into anti-Semitism?

00:57:19.320 --> 00:57:22.260
I'm just wondering if you
could explain a little bit

00:57:22.260 --> 00:57:25.800
how the hijackers saw that difference

00:57:25.800 --> 00:57:28.350
and how you deal with it in the book?

00:57:28.350 --> 00:57:30.540
- Yeah, so thank you for that, Maury.

00:57:30.540 --> 00:57:33.780
The Popular Front was very
clear that they distinguished

00:57:33.780 --> 00:57:35.970
between Jews and Zionists.

00:57:35.970 --> 00:57:37.980
So this is what they said, I mean,

00:57:37.980 --> 00:57:39.750
you can take it as you wish,

00:57:39.750 --> 00:57:42.240
but it is their position papers.

00:57:42.240 --> 00:57:45.090
Habash, who I mentioned before,
their founder and leader,

00:57:46.200 --> 00:57:47.490
when the hijacking happened,

00:57:47.490 --> 00:57:50.790
he had recently said to
a reporter that the PFLP

00:57:50.790 --> 00:57:54.150
was quote, "not hostile
to the Jews as Jews

00:57:54.150 --> 00:57:56.220
and did not aim at annihilating them

00:57:56.220 --> 00:57:58.140
or throwing them into the sea."

00:57:58.140 --> 00:58:01.057
So, and further quote,

00:58:01.057 --> 00:58:03.030
"we harbor no hostility to the Jews,

00:58:03.030 --> 00:58:04.440
but we shall fight the Zionists

00:58:04.440 --> 00:58:06.930
because they invaded and
occupied our homeland."

00:58:06.930 --> 00:58:08.790
Now obviously, that's
a very loaded statement

00:58:08.790 --> 00:58:10.020
for many people.

00:58:10.020 --> 00:58:14.880
I will say though, that
from reading interviews

00:58:14.880 --> 00:58:16.413
with my fellow hostages,

00:58:17.430 --> 00:58:21.210
many, some or many of them seem
to come to an understanding

00:58:21.210 --> 00:58:22.110
of that during the week.

00:58:22.110 --> 00:58:25.410
So, you know, one person told
a reporter, well, you know,

00:58:25.410 --> 00:58:27.540
they like the Jews, but they
don't like the Zionists.

00:58:27.540 --> 00:58:29.190
Or, you know, they said they
could live with the Jews

00:58:29.190 --> 00:58:30.843
as long as they were non-Zionist.

00:58:31.980 --> 00:58:34.330
And so, you know, that distinction isn't

00:58:35.190 --> 00:58:36.540
one that everybody would make,

00:58:36.540 --> 00:58:39.780
but the thing that was interesting was

00:58:39.780 --> 00:58:44.040
when our captors asked us that
one night when they asked,

00:58:44.040 --> 00:58:46.490
you know, what's your
religion or are you Jewish?

00:58:47.430 --> 00:58:49.800
You know, that was something that in a way

00:58:49.800 --> 00:58:51.540
was a tactical error.

00:58:51.540 --> 00:58:54.510
And in the book I quote
this man, William Quandt,

00:58:54.510 --> 00:58:56.010
who was from the National
Security Council,

00:58:56.010 --> 00:58:57.880
who said, you know, he
put it this way he said,

00:58:57.880 --> 00:58:59.430
"You know, the PFLP didn't go around

00:58:59.430 --> 00:59:01.410
and ask people about
their political views,

00:59:01.410 --> 00:59:03.510
they simply asked what
their religion was,"

00:59:03.510 --> 00:59:05.820
which was, quote, "not
a very smart thing to do

00:59:05.820 --> 00:59:07.920
if you're trying to combat the common view

00:59:07.920 --> 00:59:09.267
of anti-Semitism."

00:59:10.260 --> 00:59:11.790
And he's absolutely right.

00:59:11.790 --> 00:59:14.220
But what is interesting
is in those I learned

00:59:14.220 --> 00:59:16.530
during my research in the interrogations

00:59:16.530 --> 00:59:18.780
of various adults on the plane,

00:59:18.780 --> 00:59:21.720
they did actually ask people
their views on Zionism

00:59:21.720 --> 00:59:23.550
as a way to determine who they felt

00:59:23.550 --> 00:59:24.950
would be a valuable hostage.

00:59:25.800 --> 00:59:30.180
So, you know, that I did a lot
of reading on anti-Zionism,

00:59:30.180 --> 00:59:31.770
anti-Semitism.

00:59:31.770 --> 00:59:34.500
I have my own view on it,
not everybody will agree,

00:59:34.500 --> 00:59:36.210
it's quite fraught.

00:59:36.210 --> 00:59:39.900
But I do think it was
very important for me

00:59:39.900 --> 00:59:42.153
to read the literature of my captors,

00:59:43.380 --> 00:59:44.730
and at least to have a sense

00:59:44.730 --> 00:59:47.730
of what their official positions were.

00:59:47.730 --> 00:59:49.762
And you know, I've also
talked to people who said,

00:59:49.762 --> 00:59:51.570
you know, they shouldn't,
you know, people,

00:59:51.570 --> 00:59:52.740
they shouldn't have
asked you that question.

00:59:52.740 --> 00:59:55.410
It was just kind of like a
flawed human being thing to do.

00:59:55.410 --> 00:59:58.530
Like people don't always follow
their political strategies,

00:59:58.530 --> 01:00:00.870
you know, in the perfect way.

01:00:00.870 --> 01:00:03.870
And I do think they
understood after that night

01:00:03.870 --> 01:00:05.310
that it was the wrong thing to do.

01:00:05.310 --> 01:00:08.061
So complicated, again-

01:00:08.061 --> 01:00:11.250
- And there was also the
confusion that they would refer,

01:00:11.250 --> 01:00:14.520
they didn't wanna say the
word Israeli sometimes

01:00:14.520 --> 01:00:16.260
to acknowledge Israel.

01:00:16.260 --> 01:00:18.930
So they would call people Jews,

01:00:18.930 --> 01:00:20.850
even though they really meant Israelis.

01:00:20.850 --> 01:00:22.290
And so that-
- Also, you know,

01:00:22.290 --> 01:00:26.190
very confusing and
frightening to some people.

01:00:26.190 --> 01:00:29.520
So language, I do talk
about language in the book,

01:00:29.520 --> 01:00:32.310
and I actually, it's so
important that I invoke

01:00:32.310 --> 01:00:34.800
a quotation from the Little
Prince talking about,

01:00:34.800 --> 01:00:36.870
you know, I don't know
the exact quotation now,

01:00:36.870 --> 01:00:40.290
but that language can be
confusing and can cause harm.

01:00:40.290 --> 01:00:42.970
And so all of those aspects

01:00:44.460 --> 01:00:47.130
made it in a way, and maybe this is,

01:00:47.130 --> 01:00:48.390
you decide if it's an ending point,

01:00:48.390 --> 01:00:51.720
but in a way, those
complexities and nuances

01:00:51.720 --> 01:00:55.050
made it possible for people
to understand the event

01:00:55.050 --> 01:00:58.683
in whatever context they
brought to it before it began.

01:01:00.417 --> 01:01:02.100
- Yeah, so interesting.

01:01:02.100 --> 01:01:05.400
Okay we are out of time
and I apologize to everyone

01:01:05.400 --> 01:01:08.730
whose questions I didn't get to,

01:01:08.730 --> 01:01:10.530
but this was such, I
think it's just a sign

01:01:10.530 --> 01:01:12.450
that it's such a rich book

01:01:12.450 --> 01:01:15.780
and I really urge everyone to read it.

01:01:15.780 --> 01:01:17.160
And thank you.

01:01:17.160 --> 01:01:19.500
You can't hear everyone clapping,

01:01:19.500 --> 01:01:20.970
but I will clap for you.

01:01:20.970 --> 01:01:23.010
So thank you so much, Martha,

01:01:23.010 --> 01:01:26.124
for sharing this with us today.

01:01:26.124 --> 01:01:27.249
- Thank you so much Maury,

01:01:27.249 --> 01:01:29.190
and I would like to thank
the audience, all of you,

01:01:29.190 --> 01:01:32.040
for all of your very
thought-provoking questions.

01:01:32.040 --> 01:01:32.873
Thank you so much.

01:01:32.873 --> 01:01:34.200
- Yeah, thank you to the audience

01:01:34.200 --> 01:01:37.740
and I hope to see you
at future YPSA events.

01:01:37.740 --> 01:01:40.740
So again, please check
out our calendar events

01:01:40.740 --> 01:01:44.700
on our website and sign
up for our mailing list.

01:01:44.700 --> 01:01:46.083
Okay, bye everybody.

